{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AC1 measured. Run 44526811291 (main at f8e9ea0e61f, which contains step 1 from Gerrit 3455), `sweep (windows)` job 135569438854; the test passed this time. `[rebar-ci-timing]` spans for test_write_survives_foreground_maintenance_repack:\n- write_paths 10.1 s\n- hash_object[0..9]: 26.4, 24.8, 37.2, 28.7, 26.1, 61.5, 57.6, 63.1, 63.9, 55.7 s (445 s total)\n- unlink_paths 8.2 s\n- create_tickets 56.9 s\n\nPer-object cost: 445 s / 20,000 objects = 22 ms per object on Windows, against about 57 µs on macOS (about 390 times slower). git was progressing, not blocked: every chunk completed, and none dominated. The second half runs about 2.2 times slower per chunk than the first, so the cost grows as loose objects accumulate. The call phase therefore totals about 520 s against the 600 s budget, so normal runner variance is enough to cross it. That explains the intermittent timeouts.\n\nCause: the 20,000-object loose fill costs per-object NTFS time; there is no pipe stall. Step 2 (replace the fill with the minimal threshold crossing) follows."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1795097382089264276,"uuid":"85752a01-a15a-4c3a-9bf1-511376df36dd"}